1	//! `reframe_clip {follow: speaker}` (road map "creator-jobs" VR6a, spec §11
2	//! VR-60, VR-61), pure: a wide two-person clip framed on the face whose
3	//! microphone is speaking. The app hands the op who speaks when
4	//! (`subjects.turns`, Switch by Speaker's reading of the microphones) and
5	//! each face's mouth opening (`mouth`); `reframe_speaker_match.rs` matches
6	//! faces to microphones (its items re-exported here), and this module turns
7	//! the turns into held segments and writes them as cuts through
8	//! `reframe_path.rs`'s `write_keys`.
9	//!
10	//! - **Segments** (VR-61 (e)). Cut at the turns' bounds: one speaker frames
11	//!   their face, nobody holds the last (the first labelled one's at the
12	//!   start), two or more frame the pair (the face already framed first) when
13	//!   both widened median boxes fit the window, else the face already
14	//!   framed; equal neighbours coalesce, and the earliest segment shorter
15	//!   than [`SPEAKER_HOLD`] joins the one before it (the first, the one
16	//!   after) until none is short.
17	//! - **The path** (VR-61 (f)). Each segment start after 0 is a hard cut (a
18	//!   shot to `write_keys`), every sample choosing its segment's face, the
19	//!   pair's other face as `second`.
20	//!
21	//! VR-60 (d)'s sentences are all here: [`mics_refusal`] (the subject-free
22	//! ones, which `edit_reframe.rs` asks after (5)–(6)) and
23	//! [`plan_speaker_framing`]'s `Err`s (those that need `subjects`), each in
24	//! its order.
25	
